      "role": "user",
      "content": "Answer the following question with a single MySQL SQL query against the current database. Explore the semantic network and verify with mysql_query as needed. The SELECT list must contain exactly the columns the question asks for, in the asked order. When the final SQL is verified, call the submit_SQL tool with it.\n\nThe following hints are provided to help you write the SQL:\n\n## Gold Tables\n- SIS_SUBJECT_CODE\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT\n- SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY\n- SIS_ADMIN_DEPARTMENT\n- CIS_COURSE_CATALOG\n\n## Column Mapping\n- departments: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE, SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_NAME\n- Mathematics department: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_NAME\n- total number of enrolled students: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS\n- average total enrolled students across all departments: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS, SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- degree-granting departments: SIS_DEPARTMENT.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING\n- Electrical Engineering and Computer Science: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- courses listed in the course catalog: CIS_COURSE_CATALOG.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- unique course titles: CIS_COURSE_CATALOG.SUBJECT_TITLE\n- department code: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- department name: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_NAME\n\n## Join Keys\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE <-> SIS_SUBJECT_CODE.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE <-> SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.OFFER_DEPT_CODE\n- SIS_ADMIN_DEPARTMENT.SIS_ADMIN_DEPARTMENT_CODE <-> SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- CIS_COURSE_CATALOG.DEPARTMENT_CODE <-> SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n\n## Domain Knowledge\n- \"math\" is predicated by \"TABLE.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics'\"\n- \"degree-granting\" is predicated by \"TABLE.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING = 'Y'\"\n- \"Course 18\" is predicated by \"TABLE.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics'\"\n- \"Electrical Engineering and Computer Science\" is predicated by \"TABLE.DEPARTMENT_CODE = '6'\"\n\n## Subquery Decomposition\n1. For each department, provide the department code and the total number of enrolled students in all subjects offered by that department, considering only those departments where the total number of enrolled students is more than one standard deviation above the average total enrolled students across all departments, and restrict your answer to the Mathematics department (Course 18).\n```sql\nSELECT sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE, SUM(sos.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS) AS total_enrolled_students FROM SIS_DEPARTMENT sd JOIN SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY sos ON sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sos.OFFER_DEPT_CODE JOIN SIS_SUBJECT_CODE ssc ON ssc.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE WHERE sd.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics' GROUP BY sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE HAVING SUM(sos.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS) > ( SELECT AVG(total_enrolled) + STDDEV(total_enrolled) FROM ( SELECT SUM(sos2.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS) AS total_enrolled FROM SIS_DEPARTMENT sd2 JOIN SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY sos2 ON sd2.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sos2.OFFER_DEPT_CODE JOIN SIS_SUBJECT_CODE ssc2 ON ssc2.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sd2.DEPARTMENT_CODE GROUP BY sd2.DEPARTMENT_CODE ) t );\n```\n2. Which three departments that grant degrees have the highest number of courses listed in the course catalog, and what are their department codes and department names? Exclude the Electrical Engineering and Computer Science department (department code '6').\n```sql\nSELECT sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE, sd.DEPARTMENT_NAME, COUNT(*) AS num_courses FROM SIS_DEPARTMENT sd JOIN CIS_COURSE_CATALOG ccc ON sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE = ccc.DEPARTMENT_CODE WHERE sd.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING = 'Y' AND sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE <> '6' GROUP BY sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE, sd.DEPARTMENT_NAME ORDER BY num_courses DESC LIMIT 3;\n```\n3. For each department, provide the department code, department name, and the total number of enrolled students in all subjects offered by that department, considering only those departments where the total number of enrolled students is more than one standard deviation above the average total enrolled students across all departments, and restrict your answer to the Mathematics department (Course 18).\n```sql\nWITH inner_cte AS ( SELECT sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE, sd.DEPARTMENT_NAME, SUM(sos.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS) AS total_enrolled_students FROM SIS_DEPARTMENT sd JOIN SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY sos ON sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sos.OFFER_DEPT_CODE JOIN SIS_SUBJECT_CODE ssc ON ssc.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE WHERE sd.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics' GROUP BY sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE, sd.DEPARTMENT_NAME HAVING SUM(sos.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS) > ( SELECT AVG(total_enrolled) + STDDEV(total_enrolled) FROM ( SELECT SUM(sos2.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS) AS total_enrolled FROM SIS_DEPARTMENT sd2 JOIN SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY sos2 ON sd2.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sos2.OFFER_DEPT_CODE JOIN SIS_SUBJECT_CODE ssc2 ON ssc2.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sd2.DEPARTMENT_CODE GROUP BY sd2.DEPARTMENT_CODE ) t ) ) SELECT DEPARTMENT_CODE, DEPARTMENT_NAME, total_enrolled_students FROM inner_cte;\n```\n4. For departments that grant degrees and have at least 5 courses listed in the course catalog, excluding the Electrical Engineering and Computer Science department (department code '6'), what are the department codes, department names, and the total number of unique course titles they offer?\n```sql\nWITH inner_cte AS ( SELECT sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE, sd.DEPARTMENT_NAME, COUNT(*) AS num_courses FROM SIS_DEPARTMENT sd JOIN CIS_COURSE_CATALOG ccc ON sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE = ccc.DEPARTMENT_CODE WHERE sd.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING = 'Y' AND sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE <> '6' GROUP BY sd.DEPARTMENT_CODE, sd.DEPARTMENT_NAME ) SELECT ic.DEPARTMENT_CODE, ic.DEPARTMENT_NAME, COUNT(DISTINCT ccc.SUBJECT_TITLE) AS unique_course_titles FROM inner_cte ic JOIN CIS_COURSE_CATALOG ccc ON ic.DEPARTMENT_CODE = ccc.DEPARTMENT_CODE WHERE ic.num_courses >= 5 GROUP BY ic.DEPARTMENT_CODE, ic.DEPARTMENT_NAME ORDER BY unique_course_titles DESC;\n```\n\n## Question\nList all departments that either (1) are the Mathematics department with a total number of enrolled students in all subjects offered by that department more than one standard deviation (using STDDEV only and never STDDEV_POP) above the average total enrolled students across all departments, or (2) are degree-granting departments (excluding Electrical Engineering and Computer Science) with at least 5 courses listed in the course catalog, along with the department code, department name, the total number of enrolled students (for the Mathematics department case), the total number of unique course titles (for the degree-granting case), and a reason for inclusion (\"High Enrollment Mathematics\" or \"Degree-Granting with Many Courses\"). The columns should be: department code, department name, total number of enrolled students, total number of unique course titles, and reason for inclusion, in that order. Do not return any rounded answers."
